--TEST--
`Core\Reflect\ClassInfo::call` judges `$arguments` against the callee's declared parameters — both sides of the arity bound, and a type the parameter does not accept — `rule:security/reflection-enforces-visibility`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// covers: Core\Reflect\ClassInfo::call
// `rule:security/reflection-enforces-visibility`'s check is the ordinary call's, so what the argument list is
// judged against is the *callee's* shape and never the array's. Both sides of
// each bound are named here: a member that stopped one entry early would print
// plausibly against either half alone.
class Ledger {
    public function post(string $who, int $amount): string {
        return $who . " posted " . ($amount as string);
    }

    public function seal(): string {
        return "sealed";
    }
}

var $ledger = new Ledger();
var $described = Core\Reflect::forObject($ledger);

// Exactly the declared count, in declaration order.
echo $described->call($ledger, "post", ["ana", 3]), "\n";

// One short is refused — the first list the callee cannot be entered with.
try {
    var $short = $described->call($ledger, "post", ["ana"]);
    echo "short call", "\n";
} catch (LogicError $tooFew) {
    echo "too few", "\n";
}

// One over is not: an argument nothing declares has no parameter to be
// checked against, so the list is trimmed to the arity exactly as an ordinary
// erased call trims it. This is the accepted side of the same bound.
echo $described->call($ledger, "post", ["ben", 4, "ignored"]), "\n";

// A zero-parameter method takes `[]`, which is the only spelling for it:
// `$arguments` is required because the registry has no array default to give
// it, and `crate::reflect`'s row says so.
echo $described->call($ledger, "seal", []), "\n";

// An entry whose type the parameter does not accept is refused at the same
// check, before anything jumps — the reflective path never widens what an
// ordinary call would not.
try {
    var $wrong = $described->call($ledger, "post", ["cass", "5"]);
    echo "mistyped call", "\n";
} catch (LogicError $refused) {
    echo "wrong type", "\n";
}
--EXPECT--
ana posted 3
too few
ben posted 4
sealed
wrong type
